// apps/workflows — Workers Builds ANCHOR CONFIG. Not a runtime Worker.
//
// This app is a Trigger.dev v3/v4 task project: the tasks run on Trigger.dev's
// Node runner, never inside workerd (root CLAUDE.md §6). It has no Worker
// surface and must never grow one.
//
// This file exists for exactly one reason: Cloudflare Workers Builds is
// configured per *Worker*, so a Worker record must exist for the build trigger
// to attach to. `main` points at ./ci/worker-stub.ts, a 404 handler that is
// deployed ONCE by hand to create that record and then never again — the
// Workers Builds deploy command for this app is `trigger.dev deploy`, not
// `wrangler deploy`.
//
// ONE-TIME BOOTSTRAP (per account):
//   pnpm --filter @baseout/workflows exec wrangler deploy
//
// There are deliberately NO `env` blocks. Every Baseout Worker uses the bare
// name in both accounts (staging account vs the separate production account —
// see apps/server/wrangler.jsonc), and Workers Builds overrides the name via
// WRANGLER_CI_OVERRIDE_NAME anyway. The staging/production split for this app
// lives entirely in the build trigger's deploy command
// (`--env staging` vs `--env prod` on the Trigger.dev side), so per-env
// wrangler config would be dead weight that could only drift.
//
// NO bindings, NO vars, NO secrets. Task runtime config comes from
// process.env, populated in Trigger.dev's environment-variables UI per
// environment (README.md "Runtime"). Nothing this app needs is reachable from
// a Cloudflare binding.
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "baseout-workflows",
  "main": "./ci/worker-stub.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-22",
  "workers_dev": false,
  "observability": {
    "enabled": false
  }
}
